          "id": "2b",
          "label": "(b)",
          "text": "Cholera is an example of an infectious disease.\nExplain what is meant by an infectious disease.\n\nThe symptoms of cholera are caused by choleragen, a toxin released by the bacterium.\nCholeragen is a protein made up of six polypeptides:\n• a single copy of a polypeptide known as the A subunit that includes an extended alpha\nhelix\n• five polypeptides that together make the B subunit.\nThe B subunit of choleragen binds to a cell surface membrane component, known as GM1, of an\nintestinal epithelial cell. The complete choleragen protein then enters the cell by endocytosis. Once\ninside the cell, the A subunit of the protein acts as an enzyme, disrupting the normal functioning of\nthe cell.",
          "marks": 2,
          "type": "explain",
          "answerType": "long_answer",
          "hasDiagramRef": false,
          "diagramOnly": false,
          "parts": []
        },
        {
          "id": "2c",
          "label": "(c)",
          "text": "List the levels of protein structure present in choleragen.",
          "marks": 2,
          "type": "list",
          "answerType": "short_answer",
          "hasDiagramRef": false,
          "diagramOnly": false,
          "parts": []
        },
        {
          "id": "2d",
          "label": "(d)",
          "text": "Outline the mechanism by which choleragen enters the cell.\nYou may use the space for annotated diagrams.",
          "marks": 3,
          "type": "outline",
          "answerType": "long_answer",
          "hasDiagramRef": false,
          "diagramOnly": false,
          "parts": []
        },
        {
